      "writtenOn": "2021-07-02T08:54:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am afraid flash write size DOES mean file size alignment necessary.\n\nWhen RO is asked by HOST to update RW, flash_command_write() is called so we have the following main processes inside:\n\n\tflash_command_write()\n\t\tflash_command_write()\n\t\t\tflash_range_ok()\n\nIn flash_range_ok(), we see three arguments: offset, size and CONFIG_FLASH_WRITE_SIZE. If RW image file size is not aligned with CONFIG_FLASH_WRITE_SIZE, flash_range_ok() returns 0. More precisely in this case, flash_range_ok() returns 0 because the following check fails:\n\n\t(offset | size_req) \u0026 (align - 1)\n\nTaking a real case as shown in #14 of BUG\u003db:188117811 for example, RW image size \u003d 121036 bytes and CONFIG_FLASH_WRITE_SIZE \u003d 8. To update this RW, flash_command_write will be executed several times and each time flash_command_write is asked to program 528-byte except the last run. In the last run of flash_command_write, only 124-byte remains and this is where flash_range_ok returns 0. Because 124-byte ends up 4-byte remained which is not able to be programmed when programming unit is CONFIG_FLASH_WRITE_SIZE \u003d 8.\n\nThis is the reason why I think alignment necessary. Back to your question of stm32l15x, I would change CONFIG_FLASH_WRITE_SIZE to 0x04 from 0x0080 according to datasheet. I am not sure why it is 0x0080 for stm32l15x. If it was really 0x0080, however, the file size NEEDs to be 0x0080 alignment based on my explanation above.",
